<h2>Part 3. Choosing the Right Vehicle by Group Size</h2>

<h3>Step one: count the people honestly</h3>
<p>The most common mistake is to book for the number you hope to carry instead of the number who will turn up. Ask the organiser for a name list. If the count is within two of a vehicle's limit, consider the next size up, because an extra seat for luggage or a late addition costs far less than a second vehicle.</p>

<h3>Step two: decide what matters more, cost or comfort</h3>
<p>Every size has a value option and a comfort option. For 12 people, that is the Maharaja Tempo Traveller or the Maharaja Urbania. For 17, the Tempo Traveller or the Urbania. From 21 seats up, the choice is about size, since mini buses and tourist coaches are priced by seat count.</p>

<h3>Step three: match the vehicle to the road</h3>
<p>Hill roads, forest sections and narrow resort lanes favour smaller, nimbler vehicles. The Urbania and the Tempo Traveller handle ghat roads to Coorg, Ooty, Wayanad and Chikmagalur comfortably. Large coaches are better suited to highways and well-built roads, and they need proper parking at the destination. Before you book a large bus, ask whether your resort or temple can accept it.</p>

