<h3>What the numbers tell you</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Full vehicles are cheaper per person.</strong> The bigger the vehicle you can actually fill, the lower the cost per head. A 40 seater bus with 40 people costs far less per person than a 12 seater with 12.</li>
<li><strong>The Tempo Traveller and the Urbania are close at 17 people.</strong> The 17 seater Urbania costs about ₹283 more per person than the Tempo Traveller on a two-day Coorg trip. That is the price of the quieter cabin and the extras.</li>
<li><strong>Half-empty vehicles are expensive.</strong> A 17 seater Urbania carrying 9 people costs about ₹2,689 per person on the same trip. Choose the vehicle by how many people you will really carry.</li>
<li><strong>Mid-size groups are well served by mini buses.</strong> For 18 to 25 people, a mini bus avoids the cost of two Tempo Travellers and the awkwardness of splitting a group.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why one big vehicle often beats two small ones</h3>
<p>Two 12 seater vehicles cost two daily Batas, two sets of tolls and require two drivers to coordinate. A single 21 seater mini bus keeps a group of 20 together at a lower total cost and removes the problem of splitting friends and families. Splitting is cheapest only when the groups travel to different destinations.</p>

<h2>Part 5. How a Quotation Is Built</h2>
<p>A written quotation from Yogi Tours &amp; Travels is itemised so you can check every line. Understanding the terms lets you compare it fairly with any other quotation you receive.</p>

<h3>The terms in plain language</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Per-km rate.</strong> The price per kilometre of driving, for the vehicle and the driver's time on the road.</li>
<li><strong>Daily minimum kilometres.</strong> The least distance billed per day. For Tempo Travellers and Urbanias this is 300 km. If you drive less, 300 km is billed. It is not a penalty. It reflects the fact that the vehicle and driver are committed to you for the day.</li>
<li><strong>Driver Bata.</strong> The daily allowance for the driver's food and incidentals, charged per day of duty. It is ₹500 for the 9 and 12 seater Tempo Travellers and ₹700 for the 17 seater Tempo Traveller and both Urbanias.</li>
<li><strong>Duty hours.</strong> Standard duty runs from 6:00 AM to 10:00 PM. Extra driver Bata applies for duty after 10:00 PM.</li>
<li><strong>Extras at actuals.</strong> Tolls, parking, interstate permits and state taxes are billed at the real amounts paid.</li>
</ul>

<h3>How billed distance works</h3>
<p>Billed distance is the larger of the actual round-trip distance and the daily minimum for the number of days. A one-day trip to Mysore and back is about 290 km, so 300 km is billed. A two-day trip to Coorg is about 510 km, so the 600 km minimum is billed. A three-day trip to Hampi is about 690 km plus local driving, so the 900 km minimum is billed. If your trip includes a lot of local sightseeing, the actual distance may exceed the minimum, and then the actual distance is billed.</p>

<h3>Questions to ask any operator</h3>
<ol>
<li>What is the exact seat count of the vehicle I will get?</li>
<li>What is the per-km rate, the daily minimum and the driver Bata?</li>
<li>What is billed at actuals, and how will I see the receipts?</li>
<li>Is the rate for AC or non-AC?</li>
<li>What happens if the trip runs late into the night?</li>
<li>What is the cancellation policy?</li>
<li>Can I see a photo of the actual vehicle?</li>
</ol>
<p>A written quotation that answers all of these is a sign of a transparent operator. We put all of them in writing before you pay. See our <a href="/cancellation-policy">cancellation policy</a>.</p>

<h2>Part 6. Permits, Taxes and Paperwork</h2>
<p>Travelling by tourist vehicle across state lines involves permits and taxes, which are a common source of confusion and of surprise bills. This is a plain-language summary. Your quotation lists the specifics for your route.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tourist permit.</strong> Tourist vehicles run under a tourist permit. We operate under the All India Tourist Permit, which is designed to allow tourist vehicles to travel between states, subject to the taxes and fees each state charges.</li>
<li><strong>State entry taxes.</strong> When a vehicle enters another state, such as Tamil Nadu, Kerala, Andhra Pradesh or Goa, that state may charge a tax or fee. These are billed at actuals.</li>
<li><strong>Tolls.</strong> National and state highway tolls are billed at actuals. Many routes from Bangalore have several toll plazas.</li>
<li><strong>Parking.</strong> At temples, resorts and city destinations, parking fees are billed at actuals.</li>
<li><strong>Forest entry and restrictions.</strong> Some routes cross forest areas with entry timings or night closures, such as the stretches near Bandipur and Mudumalai on the way to Ooty. Planning around these is part of a driver's job.</li>
<li><strong>GST invoice.</strong> Companies can request a GST invoice. Give us the company details before the trip.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Part 7. Group Trip Planning, Case by Case</h2>

<h3>Weddings and family functions</h3>
<p>Weddings need several vehicles on a tight timetable. Use the Maharaja Urbania for the immediate family and honoured guests, the 17 seater Urbania or Tempo Traveller for larger family groups, and mini buses for the wider guest list. Write a movement timetable, share it with us and the coordinator, and leave buffers. See <a href="/services/wedding-transportation">wedding transportation</a>.</p>

<h3>Corporate offsites, conferences and delegations</h3>
<p>For small teams, use an Urbania. For departments and larger teams, use a 21 or 25 seater mini bus. For conferences with a large attendance, use a combination of vehicles with a single point of contact. Provide the guest list, the flight or train details and the venue address in advance. We issue GST invoices. See <a href="/services/corporate-travel">corporate travel</a> and <a href="/services/event-transportation">event transportation</a>.</p>

<h3>School and college trips</h3>
<p>Educational tours need larger vehicles and strong safety planning. The 33, 40 and 50 seater tourist buses are used for school and college excursions, with experienced commercial drivers. Share the list of teachers and students, the itinerary and the timings, and agree the supervision plan with the institution before departure. See <a href="/services/educational-tours">educational tours</a>.</p>

<h3>Pilgrimages</h3>
<p>Tirupati, Mantralaya, Dharmasthala, Kukke Subramanya and similar routes involve early starts, long drives and elderly travellers. The 17 seater Tempo Traveller, the 17 seater Urbania, mini buses and 40 seater buses cover the usual group sizes. Book darshan and accommodation in advance, carry original IDs and plan driver rest. See <a href="/services/pilgrimage-tours">pilgrimage tours</a>.</p>

<h3>Airport transfers for groups</h3>
<p>A group of 10 to 17 arriving at Kempegowda International Airport is best served by one vehicle that meets everyone at once. Share the flight number and arrival time, and agree the meeting point. See <a href="/services/airport-transfer">airport transfer</a> and our <a href="/blog/bangalore-airport-taxi-cab-guide-kempegowda-blr-transfers">airport taxi guide</a>.</p>

<h3>Family tours and resort stays</h3>
<p>For multi-generational holidays, pick a vehicle with space for the luggage and comfort for the oldest traveller. Check that the resort accepts the vehicle's size at its gate and car park. See <a href="/services/family-tours">family tours</a> and <a href="/services/resort-trips">resort trips</a>.</p>

