<h2>Part 8B. Mini Buses and Tourist Buses: What to Know Before You Book</h2>
<p>Once your group passes 17 people, an Urbania is no longer an option and the choice is between mini buses and tourist coaches. They behave differently from vans, and a few questions up front save a lot of trouble.</p>

<h3>Mini buses (21 and 25 seats)</h3>
<p>The <a href="/fleet/mini-bus/mini-bus-21-seater">21 seater</a> and <a href="/fleet/mini-bus/mini-bus-25-seater">25 seater</a> mini buses have air conditioning, push-back seats, reading lights, a PA system, a first-aid kit and overhead luggage racks, plus a rear boot. They are large enough for a department or a wedding party and small enough to manage city roads and hill sections that a full coach cannot.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Luggage:</strong> overhead racks suit soft bags, and the rear boot takes larger cases. Very large cases can be a tight fit, so tell us the count.</li>
<li><strong>Roads:</strong> mini buses take highways and most hill roads comfortably. Narrow lanes to a remote resort may be difficult, so ask your resort.</li>
<li><strong>Announcements:</strong> the PA system helps a coordinator brief the group without shouting, which suits school groups and tours.</li>
<li><strong>Cost:</strong> ₹34 per km for 21 seats and ₹38 per km for 25 seats. The daily minimum and Bata are confirmed in the quotation.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Tourist buses (33, 40 and 50 seats)</h3>
<p>The <a href="/fleet/tourist-bus/tourist-bus-33-seater">33 seater</a>, <a href="/fleet/tourist-bus/tourist-bus-40-seater">40 seater</a> and <a href="/fleet/tourist-bus/tourist-bus-50-seater">50 seater</a> tourist buses are for large group tours, college and school excursions and big events, with push-back seats, air conditioning and ample luggage space. They are the most economical way to move a large group per person.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Parking and access:</strong> a large coach needs a proper parking area at temples, resorts and sightseeing spots. Ask your destination in advance.</li>
<li><strong>Turning and narrow roads:</strong> a 50 seater cannot reach every hill resort. For steep or narrow final approaches, a smaller vehicle may shuttle the group from a drop point.</li>
<li><strong>Night driving:</strong> large coaches on long routes need driver rest. A long overnight run may need two drivers. Ask what is planned.</li>
<li><strong>Cost:</strong> ₹45, ₹50 and ₹60 per km for 33, 40 and 50 seats respectively. Daily terms are confirmed in the quotation.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Questions to ask about any bus</h3>
<ol>
<li>How many seats does the vehicle really have, and what is the seating layout?</li>
<li>Is it air-conditioned throughout the journey, and what does that mean for the rate?</li>
<li>Can it reach my destination, and where will it park?</li>
<li>What is the daily minimum, and how is the night driving charged?</li>
<li>Who is the driver, and is there a relief driver on long trips?</li>
<li>What are the permits and taxes for my route?</li>
</ol>

