<h2>Part 8E. Common Mistakes When Hiring a Group Vehicle</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Comparing only the per-km rate.</strong> A lower rate with a higher minimum, extra Bata or hidden extras can cost more. Compare the total.</li>
<li><strong>Choosing by name, not by seats.</strong> Ask for the exact seat count and layout. A vehicle marketed as a 17 seater may carry fewer passengers in comfort.</li>
<li><strong>Booking a vehicle that is too small.</strong> If your group could grow, book for the larger number.</li>
<li><strong>Booking a vehicle that is too large.</strong> Half-empty vehicles cost more per person.</li>
<li><strong>Skipping the written quotation.</strong> A phone price is not a contract. Ask for the terms in writing.</li>
<li><strong>Forgetting interstate permits.</strong> Tell the operator your full route so permits and taxes are included.</li>
<li><strong>Underestimating travel time.</strong> Groups stop more often and for longer. Plan for it.</li>
<li><strong>Driving too late.</strong> Late-night driving tires the driver and adds Bata. Plan to finish early.</li>
<li><strong>Not checking access.</strong> Large vehicles cannot enter every lane. Ask your resort, temple or venue.</li>
<li><strong>Ignoring reviews.</strong> Read reviews carefully, as described below.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Part 8F. How to Check Any Travel Operator</h2>
<p>Whoever you book with, a few checks tell you a lot.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Read reviews for details.</strong> Look for reviews that mention the vehicle type, the route and the driver by name, and that have different dates and different writing styles. Many identical five-star reviews on one day are a warning sign.</li>
<li><strong>Check that the numbers match.</strong> The rate on the website, on the phone and in the quotation should be identical. A site that quotes different rates for the same vehicle in different places is a red flag.</li>
<li><strong>Ask for the business details.</strong> A real operator can tell you how long it has been running, where its office is and what permits its vehicles hold.</li>
<li><strong>Look at the vehicle photos.</strong> Photos of the real vehicle, from different angles and including the interior, show the operator has the vehicle.</li>
<li><strong>Test the response.</strong> Send an enquiry and see how quickly and clearly it is answered. A good first reply tells you a lot about the trip.</li>
<li><strong>Check the cancellation terms</strong> before paying an advance.</li>
</ul>
